  "learningJourneyToolQuranReaderSubtitle": "Open the reader and continue slowly.",
  "learningJourneyToolQuranStudyTitle": "Qur’an Study",
  "learningJourneyToolQuranStudySubtitle": "Use the study hub for connected practice.",
  "learningJourneyToolQuranArabicTitle": "Qur’anic Arabic",
  "learningJourneyToolQuranArabicSubtitle": "Return to the Arabic learning path.",
  "learningJourneyToolSalahHubTitle": "Salah Hub",
  "learningJourneyToolSalahHubSubtitle": "Reconnect these phrases to worship practice.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahLessonTitle": "Understanding Al-Fatihah",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahIntro": "Al-Fatihah is the surah you repeat most often in salah. Learning its core meaning turns familiar recitation into conscious worship.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahSection1Title": "A simple map of the surah",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahSection1Body": "Al-Fatihah begins with praise of Allah, then reminds you that He is Lord, Master of the Day of Judgment, and the One alone you worship and ask for help. It ends as a dua for guidance to the straight path.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahSection2Title": "How to carry it into prayer",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahSection2Body": "When you recite Al-Fatihah, slow down enough to feel the movement of the surah: praise, dependence, and asking to be guided.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahSection2Bullet1": "Praise Allah before asking for anything.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahSection2Bullet2": "Remember that worship and help both belong to Him alone.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahSection2Bullet3": "Treat the last verses as a living dua for guidance.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahTakeaway1": "Al-Fatihah is both praise and dua.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahTakeaway2": "Its meaning can make every rak‘ah more attentive.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahTakeaway3": "Understanding a little well is better than reciting without thought.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahReflection": "Which line of Al-Fatihah do you want to recite more consciously in your next prayer?",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahInvocation1Title": "Opening praise",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahInvocation1Meaning": "All praise belongs to Allah, Lord of all worlds.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteFatihahInvocation1Context": "This phrase reminds you that praise comes before requests.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsLessonTitle": "Common Phrases in Salah",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsIntro": "Salah includes short repeated phrases that shape humility, praise, and reverence. Knowing their meaning helps the body and heart move together.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsSection1Title": "What these phrases are doing",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsSection1Body": "Takbir magnifies Allah. Tasbih in ruku and sujud glorifies Him. These short phrases are not filler between positions. They are part of the inner life of salah.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsSection2Title": "How to begin using them with understanding",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsSection2Body": "Choose one phrase to pay attention to in your next prayer. Repeat it slowly enough that the meaning stays present for at least one moment.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsTakeaway1": "Short phrases can carry deep meaning inside prayer.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsTakeaway2": "Understanding grows khushu more naturally than forcing emotion.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsTakeaway3": "One attentive phrase is a strong place to begin.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsReflection": "Which phrase in salah do you want to stop rushing past?",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsInvocation1Title": "Takbir",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsInvocation1Meaning": "Allah is greater than everything competing for your attention.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsInvocation1Context": "Said when moving into major parts of the prayer.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsInvocation2Title": "Tasbih in bowing",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsInvocation2Meaning": "Glory be to my Lord, the Most Great.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsInvocation2Context": "Said in ruku to glorify Allah with humility.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsInvocation3Title": "Tasbih in prostration",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsInvocation3Meaning": "Glory be to my Lord, the Most High.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteShortSurahsInvocation3Context": "Said in sujud while you are physically lowest before Allah.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningLessonTitle": "Simple Dhikr Meanings",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningIntro": "A few short phrases appear again and again in Muslim life. When you understand them, dhikr becomes lighter to carry and easier to keep.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningSection1Title": "Start with the smallest phrases",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningSection1Body": "SubhanAllah clears Allah of نقص and imperfection. Alhamdulillah fills the heart with praise and gratitude. Short dhikr works because it can stay with you in ordinary moments.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningSection2Title": "Bridge meaning into daily rhythm",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningSection2Body": "Use one phrase after prayer, one during a quiet walk, and one when something good happens. That is enough to start a sincere daily rhythm.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningSection2Bullet1": "Say SubhanAllah when you want to remember Allah’s perfection.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningSection2Bullet2": "Say Alhamdulillah when you notice a blessing.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningSection2Bullet3": "Keep the phrases short enough to remain present and sincere.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningTakeaway1": "Short dhikr becomes stronger when you understand it.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningTakeaway2": "Meaning makes repetition more alive, not less simple.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningTakeaway3": "Daily remembrance grows best through small steady anchors.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningReflection": "Which short phrase do you want to carry more intentionally through your day?",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningInvocation1Title": "Tasbih",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningInvocation1Meaning": "Glory be to Allah.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningInvocation1Context": "Use when remembering Allah’s perfection and greatness.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningInvocation2Title": "Hamd",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningInvocation2Meaning": "All praise belongs to Allah.",
  "learningJourneyStageReciteMeaningInvocation2Context": "Use when you notice mercy, provision, or any blessing.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsOpenLessonTitle": "Harakat and Reading Clues",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsOpenIntro": "Before smooth reading comes a smaller skill: seeing the marks around the letters clearly enough to know how the sound should move.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsOpenSection1Title": "What to notice first",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsOpenSection1Body": "Harakat are the short vowel signs that tell you whether the sound opens, lowers, or rounds. Start by noticing the mark before worrying about speed.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsOpenSection1Bullet1": "Fathah usually gives a light “a” sound.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsOpenSection1Bullet2": "Kasrah usually gives a light “i” sound.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsOpenSection1Bullet3": "Dammah usually gives a light “u” sound.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsOpenSection2Title": "A gentle way to practice",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsOpenSection2Body": "Read one letter at a time, say the sound, then repeat the same pattern across two or three examples. Accuracy first. Speed later.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsOpenTakeaway1": "Reading improves when your eye sees the mark before your tongue rushes ahead.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsOpenTakeaway2": "Small accurate repetition is more useful than fast guessing.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsOpenTakeaway3": "Harakat are a doorway into confidence, not a burden.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsOpenReflection": "Which sound pattern still feels least stable for you right now?",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsPracticeLessonTitle": "Joining Letters into Reading",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsPracticeIntro": "After letter recognition, the next step is learning to stay calm when letters change shape and join together inside words.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsPracticeSection1Title": "Why joined letters feel harder",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsPracticeSection1Body": "Arabic letters can look different at the beginning, middle, or end of a word. That is normal. The goal is not instant mastery. The goal is learning to recognize the same letter in more than one form.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsPracticeSection2Title": "How to practice without overload",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsPracticeSection2Body": "Work with short groups of letters and simple words. Trace the sound from one letter to the next instead of trying to swallow the whole word at once.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsPracticeSection2Bullet1": "Pause when a familiar letter looks unfamiliar.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsPracticeSection2Bullet2": "Break the word into smaller sound groups.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsPracticeSection2Bullet3": "Repeat one short line several times before moving on.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsPracticeTakeaway1": "Joined letters become easier through pattern recognition, not force.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsPracticeTakeaway2": "Short words are enough for real progress.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsPracticeTakeaway3": "Calm repetition helps more than pushing for speed.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsPracticeReflection": "Do you need more patience with letter shapes or with sounding out words?",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsCheckpointLessonTitle": "Reading Checkpoint",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsCheckpointIntro": "A checkpoint is not a final exam. It is a quiet pause to notice what is becoming easier and what still needs one more calm pass.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsCheckpointSection1Title": "Simple self-checks",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsCheckpointSection1Body": "Use a few short checks before moving forward. You do not need perfection. You need enough clarity to keep building without confusion.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsCheckpointSection1Bullet1": "Can you identify the basic harakat without panic?",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsCheckpointSection1Bullet2": "Can you follow a short joined word more slowly and correctly?",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsCheckpointSection1Bullet3": "Do you know which sound or shape still needs extra review?",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsCheckpointSection2Title": "What to do next",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsCheckpointSection2Body": "If one area still feels weak, return to that area on purpose. Steady loops build reading confidence better than pretending you are ready for everything.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsCheckpointTakeaway1": "Checkpoints are for clarity, not discouragement.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsCheckpointTakeaway2": "Knowing your weak spot is part of progress.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsCheckpointTakeaway3": "A calm return to basics is often the fastest way forward.",
  "learningJourneyStageReadingBasicsCheckpointReflection": "Which small reading skill needs another week of focused attention before you move on?",
